这部电影是莫里斯作品的纯粹提炼，他仅用基础的形状和色彩就在讲故事、创造情感和幽默。但查克·琼斯依然得到了导演署名。而且它理所当然地赢得了奥斯卡奖，但莫里斯说查克从来没有谢过他。托德说，尽管人人都知道这是莫里斯的电影，但莫里斯并没有被邀请去参加奥斯卡颁奖典礼。所以查克·琼斯和莫里斯·诺布尔继续合作。他们合作了《圣诞怪杰》（How the Grinch Stole Christmas!）以及其他一堆苏斯博士（Dr. Seuss）作品的改编，但他们失联了大约20年，直到20世纪90年代初，华纳兄弟让他们重新聚首，制作在长片电影前播放的动画短片。他们雇佣了一群刚从加州艺术学院毕业的年轻艺术家，

23
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:46.000
who just graduated from Cal Arts, and one of them was Scott Morse. Morse, no, but Morse, no, but where do I know that from? I was like, oh, it's because it was one of the names that preceded directed by Charles M. Jones on all of the cartoons that I grew up watching every day of my life. One of the first lessons that Scott learned
其中之一就是斯科特·莫斯（Scott Morse）。莫斯，诺布尔，但莫斯，诺布尔，我怎么会知道这个名字？我当时想，哦，那是因为它是陪伴我度过生命中每一天、我从小看到大的所有动画片里，在“查尔斯·M·琼斯导演”之前出现的名字之一。斯科特学到的第一课

24
00:11:46.000 --> 00:12:02.000
was that Marisa's fantastic backgrounds were not just invented out of thin air. Marisa did a ton of research. Everything he exaggerated was based on something in the real world. You'd get an assignment like, oh, you know, Bugs Bunny's gonna be stuck on a desert island or a stuck on a jungle island.
就是，莫里斯那些光怪陆离的背景可不是凭空捏造出来的。莫里斯做了大量的调研。他夸张化的每一个细节，都是以现实世界中的事物为基础的。你会接到这样的任务：“哦，你知道的，兔八哥要被困在荒岛上了，或者被困在丛林岛上了。”

25
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:13.000
So there should be palm trees and it should be all this stuff. It's like, okay, great. And I'd come back with the drawing and he'd be like, what is this? And I'm like, are there palm trees? And he's like, those are stuckas. What were you looking at? It's like, well, there's a palm tree right out the window.
“所以这里应该有棕榈树，应该有所有这些东西。”这就像是，好的，太棒了。然后我拿着画作回来，他会说：“这是什么？”而我说：“难道这不是棕榈树吗？”他说：“那是苏铁（stuckas / cycads）。你当时到底在看什么？”我说：“好吧，窗外就有一棵棕榈树啊。”

26
00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:27.000
He's like, that's a stucka. You gotta learn your trees. But Marisa wasn't really that gruff. After work, he talked to these 22 year old animators like he was just one of the guys. He'd be like, let's go to the baseball game. Let's go do this other thing. You know, we should go to a museum.
他说：“那是苏铁。你得把树认清楚。”但莫里斯其实并没有那么粗鲁。下班后，他跟这些22岁的动画师聊天时，就像他们当中的一员一样。他会说：“我们去看棒球比赛吧。我们去干点别的什么。”“你知道的，我们应该去一趟博物馆。”

27
00:12:27.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Pretty soon you realize he's more like your grandpa who is just a really cool guy to hang out with. So Scott Morrison, the script of artists, became known as the Noble Boys. And Maurice pushed them to develop their own style, but his sensibility really seeped into their work. Like Scott is at Pixar now, which is the home of 3D animation, which we think is the complete opposite of, you know, the flat landscapes of Looney Tunes. But you can really see Marisa's influence on the Pixar films if you look for it. Like the film up. Think about that house carried by a massive amount of balloons, and lands on the very
很快你就会发现，他更像是你的爷爷，是一个非常酷、特别适合一起消磨时光的人。于是斯科特·莫斯和这群分镜画师被称为“诺布尔小子”（The Noble Boys）。莫里斯敦促他们发展自己的风格，但他的审美确实渗透到了他们的作品中。比如斯科特现在在皮克斯（Pixar），那是3D动画的大本营，在我们看来，这与《乐一通》扁平的风景完全是两个极端。但如果你仔细寻找，真的能在皮克斯的电影中看到莫里斯的影响。比如电影《飞屋环游记》（Up）。想想那座被大量气球带着飞行、最后降落在悬崖边缘的房子。

28
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:31.000
edge of a cliff. Or in Wally, this cute little robot is scooting around these big menacing towers of garbage. You know, those are layouts that make you smile. That's classic marise. In May of 2001, the noble boys were reunited one last time. We all got, you know, the call of the Maurice. He was in hospice basically at home. It was just natural causes that he, that he was dying of. And he was pretty sure, everybody's pretty sure that that night was going to maybe be the last night.
或者在《机器人总动员》（WALL-E）中，这个可爱的机器人绕着那些庞大而充满威胁的垃圾高塔四处穿梭。你知道的，那些都是能让你会心一笑的场景布局。那是经典的莫里斯风格。2001年5月，“诺布尔小子”们最后一次聚到了一起。“我们所有人都接到了莫里斯的召唤。”他当时基本上是在家中接受临终关怀。他得的其实就是自然衰老导致的疾病，生命正在走向尽头。而且他很确定，大家也都确信，那一晚可能就是最后的时刻了。

29
00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:45.000
So we all came over and hung out with him. And Chuck Jones did call that night. I remember being in the room with Maurice and somebody brought in the phone, I was like, Chuck's on the phone. And Maurice was like, OK, I'll talk to him. And we all knew for a fact that they hadn't really talked for months, if not a couple of years at that point.
于是我们都赶过去陪着他。查克·琼斯在那天晚上确实打来了电话。我记得自己当时正和莫里斯待在房间里，有人把电话拿进来，我说：“查克在电话里。”莫里斯说：“好的，我跟他说。”而我们都知道事实是，当时他们已经好几个月、甚至好几年没有真正说过话了。

30
00:13:45.000 --> 00:14:15.000
And I just heard Mori's side of the conversation, but you know, you could definitely tell that they were, they had put everything behind them and they were friends, you know, and it was nice. And I, again, I don't know exactly what Chuck said on the other end of that line, but it was a very sweet moment to be able to see that, and know that people could go through a lot together in life and still have respect for each other, you know, up to the last minute and kind of celebrate what they had created together. To some extent, Marisa the Noble Boys is his real legacy.
我只听到了莫里斯在对话中的那一头，但你可以清楚地听出，他们已经把一切都抛在脑后了，他们还是朋友，那感觉真好。再次声明，我不知道查克在电话那头具体说了什么，但能够看到这一幕，并且知道人们在生活中可以经历那么多的风雨，却依然能在最后一刻保持对彼此的尊重，并庆祝他们共同创造的成果，这是一个非常温馨的时刻。从某种程度上说，“诺布尔小子”们就是莫里斯真正的遗产。

31
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:37.000
He was proud of the fact that they had blossomed into well-rounded artists. If Maurice could leave behind this idea in other people to do their own thing, we would never be in the background like he was. But it was a beautiful, beautiful background. I actually met Marie's number once. Oh yeah? Yeah. I went to Cal Arts and he came into our class
他为他们成长为全面发展的艺术家而感到自豪。如果莫里斯能在其他人心中留下这种坚持自我风格的理念，那么他自己哪怕永远退居幕后，也值了。但他留下的背景是如此美丽，无与伦比。我其实见过一次莫里斯·诺布尔。哦，是吗？对。我去加州艺术学院念书时，他来过我们班上，

32
00:14:37.000 --> 00:15:27.000
on it, it was like a Monday night. And I had happened to have gone to the opera the day before on the weekend. And I was thinking about how often those guys made fun of opera in their cartoons. And I asked him, did you guys actually go to the opera regularly? Or were you just kind of making fun of it as a sort of you know high flutant cultural institution and he asked me what opera did you see and I said La Traviata and he said ah Velletta her death is beautiful and that is a man who knew a good death when he saw it.
那是某个周一的晚上。我刚好在前一天的周末去看了歌剧。我当时在想，这群家伙在他们的动画片里拿歌剧开玩笑的频率到底有多高。于是我问他：“你们当时是真的经常去看歌剧吗？还是说你们只是把它当成某种矫揉造作的文化机构来开玩笑？”他反问我：“你看了哪部歌剧？”我说《茶花女》（La Traviata）。他说：“啊，维奥莱塔（Violetta），她的死真美。”那真是一个懂得欣赏好死法的妙人。

